 # Sort-last Parallel Rendering
 
-Also called: Sort-Last, Sort-Last MIMD, Distributed Volume Rendering with Post-rendering Compositing
+Also called: Chromium depth composite architecture, Partitionnement d’objet, Sort-Last Distributed Rendering, Sort-Last MIMD, Sort-Last Parallel Rendering Architecture, Sort-Last Rendering, Sort-last parallelism
 
 ## Overview
-Sort-last parallel rendering is a distributed memory parallelization strategy where the dataset is decomposed spatially among processing nodes, each node renders a complete partial image of its assigned data portion at full resolution, and these partial images are composited into a final image using a parallel compositing algorithm [S1, S2][S3, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering datasets too large for single-node memory or compute capacity by leveraging the independence of local rendering tasks [S2][S6, unofficial]. Unlike [Sort-first](6265-parallelism-and-distribution.md) methods, which decompose the screen and suffer from high data sharing and load imbalance when geometry is uneven, sort-last distributes primitives or volume blocks evenly, offering superior load balancing and reduced communication costs for the geometry itself, though it incurs significant bandwidth requirements during the image compositing phase [S5][S6, unofficial].
+Sort-last parallel rendering is a distributed memory parallelization strategy where the dataset is decomposed spatially among processing nodes, each node renders a complete partial image of its assigned data portion at full resolution, and these partial images are composited into a final image using a parallel compositing algorithm [S1, S2, S3]. It solves the problem of rendering datasets too large for single-node memory or compute capacity by leveraging the independence of local rendering tasks [S2][S6, unofficial]. Unlike [Sort-first](6265-parallelism-and-distribution.md) methods, which decompose the screen and suffer from high data sharing and load imbalance when geometry is uneven, sort-last distributes primitives or volume blocks evenly, offering superior load balancing and reduced communication costs for the geometry itself, though it incurs significant bandwidth requirements during the image compositing phase [S5, S6, unofficial].
 
